  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Despite tonight being the coldest night of the \nseason so far, frost does not appear to be a threat around the Triad.\n\nA wave of low pressure is developing along the cold front that has \nmoved offshore the Carolina coast. Rain and associated cloud cover \nwith the front will continue to push east, with all of central North \nCarolina expected to be dry by sunset. Despite clearing skies, the \npressure gradient between high pressure moving over the mid-Atlantic \nstates and the developing coastal low should keep the wind around 10 \nmph overnight. The mixing of the air will prevent lows from dropping \nmuch lower than the upper 30s, which should prevent any frost \nformation.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... High pressure and a dry airmass will provide dry \nweather Sunday through next weekend. Frost not expected Sunday \nnight. \n\nWith the upper low lingering off our coast, upper ridge to our west, \nand sfc high pressure to our north which is extending south across \nour area ... we expect dry weather across central NC from Sunday \nthrough next weekend. Given persistent N/NE low level flow, temps \nwill remain several deg below normal Sunday and Monday, then holding \nnear normal for the rest of the forecast period. Frost is not \nexpected Sunday night due to lingering pressure gradient and \nresulting breeze.",
